# Expectation record for 03_initializers/007_string_array.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 007_string_array
area               = 03_initializers
description        = Character arrays initialized from string literals in both C11 6.7.9p14 forms -- exact width, where the terminator is not copied, and with room to spare, where it is -- across unsigned char, signed char and plain char, plus the braced spelling, adjacent-literal concatenation, escape sequences, a designated character array, two-dimensional grids at both widths, and a literal initializing a pointer
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
This program contains no undefined and no unspecified behaviour, which is the precondition that
makes both differential oracles sound: undefined behaviour would license either compiler to do
anything at all, so a divergence over it would prove nothing about either one.
The construct that looks most dangerous here is defined, and it is the point of the program: an
array whose declared width is exactly the length of its string literal receives the literal's bytes
and the terminating null is simply NOT copied. C11 6.7.9p14 states that explicitly, so g_exact_u[3]
from "abc", g_exact_s[3] from "xyz" and each row of g_grid_exact[2][5] from "hello" and "world" are
well defined rather than overflowing -- those, and only those, are the exact-width objects.
g_escapes[4] is deliberately NOT one of them, and saying so is worth a sentence because its literal
LOOKS longer than it is: "\x41\102\n" is three characters, measured as sizeof - 1 == 3, so the array
has a fourth element and the terminator IS copied into it, which the golden line g_escapes[3]=0
records. It is the minimal with-room case rather than an exact-width one, and mistaking it for one
would invite a maintainer to "correct" its width to 3 and delete a line of the golden record.
Nothing in the program ever reads the terminator the exact-width arrays do not have: they are never
printed with %s and never walked for a terminator, every read goes through a helper that is given
the array's DECLARED element count -- 3, 4, 5, 6 or 7 -- and loops strictly below it, and the two
grids are read with literal bounds equal to their declared row widths. No read therefore passes the
last element of any object, no subscript is ever out of bounds and no one-past-the-end pointer is
formed or dereferenced.
The `sizeof_g_exact_u=3` and `sizeof_g_grid_exact=10` lines exist to detect the failure mode that
would otherwise be silent: a compiler that helpfully reserved a byte for a terminator would either
store one past the end of the object -- an out-of-bounds write, which the sanitizer gate catches --
or size the array wrongly, which those lines catch.
Every array with room to spare is fully initialized: the literal's bytes and its terminator are
copied and every element beyond them is implicitly zero by C11 6.7.9p21, for automatic duration
exactly as for static, because an array with any initializer is fully initialized. g_desig is a
designated character array whose unmentioned elements are zero by the same rule. So no read of an
indeterminate value is reachable anywhere in the program, including from l_exact_u and l_room.
No arithmetic is performed on any character value. The only arithmetic is the loop counters'
increments, bounded by the literals 3, 4, 5, 6, 7 and 2, so signed overflow is unreachable; the
byte counts sizeof yields are constants of a complete object type. Nothing is shifted, divided or
remaindered. Nothing is modified after its initialization, so no object is modified twice between
two sequence points. Every loop bound is either a literal dimension or a count the caller passes
explicitly, so no bound is computed from a value the initializer under test supplied.
g_ptr is a const char * initialized from the literal "lit", whose array has 4 elements, and it is
read at indices 0 through 3 -- its three characters and its terminator, all inside the object the
literal denotes. No pointer VALUE is printed, no pointer difference is taken and no pointer
arithmetic beyond those in-bounds reads is performed. No string literal is modified anywhere in the
program: every literal is either an initializer for a separate mutable array of its own, or is
reached through a const-qualified pointer.
There is no aliasing violation. Each array is read only through an lvalue of its own declared element
type -- unsigned char through unsigned char, signed char through signed char, plain char through
char -- and the helpers' parameters are declared with matching element types, so no object is
accessed through an incompatible effective type. Every cast in the file is a VALUE conversion applied
to an already-loaded character, (int) or (int)(unsigned char), never a pointer cast and never pointer
punning. No object representation is inspected, no union appears, and no padding byte is read -- a
character array has no padding in any case -- and nothing depends on the relative addresses of
unrelated objects.
Each printf call receives a name pointer, one or two loop counters and one already-narrowed int,
none of which has a side effect, so the unspecified order in which a function call's arguments are
evaluated cannot be observed. %s takes a null-terminated const char * naming the array, and %d takes
an int in every case, matching after the default argument promotions, so no variadic argument is read
at the wrong width.
main returns 0, inside the 0-125 range the suite requires so that no exit status is truncated by the
operating system. Every input is a literal in this file: nothing is read from the environment, the
clock, the file system or the network, and iteration order is fixed by literal bounds, so every cell
is reproducible from this source and this record alone.
No header is included anywhere and printf is declared by hand as `int printf(const char *, ...);`.
bcc bundles the nine required freestanding headers plus a bonus stdatomic.h and ships no standard
I/O header (docs/project-guide.md line 212), so naming one would fail on the bcc side of oracle (a)
while succeeding on the reference side -- a spurious divergence caused by the test rather than by a
compiler. This area is granted no header exception; the corpus has exactly two, area 07's stdarg.h
and the dedicated bundled-header probe in area 12.
This record declares no warning-gate deviation, and area 03 sanctions none. The program is clean
under the full default gate -Wall -Wextra -pedantic -Wconversion -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow
-Werror at -O0, -O1 and -O2 with all four reference drivers, and it builds and runs clean under the
sanitizer gate -fsanitize=undefined,address -fno-sanitize-recover=all. Both gates are
reference-compiler-only and are never applied to bcc, so requirement 3's shared-flag discipline is
untouched. The unbraced string literals initializing the two grids' inner arrays are brace elision,
and they are gate-clean unaided because a string literal may initialize a character array without
braces (C11 6.7.9p14), so no pragma is needed here.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
This record narrows nothing: all four targets, all three optimization levels and all three
oracles are enabled, no expected-divergence marker is carried, and no `ub_audit_flags` key
appears because the program passes the suite's full default warning gate unmodified. That is a
stronger statement here than in the rest of the area, because this is the one program in it that
handles character data, and character data is where the corpus's two measured
implementation-defined properties live -- so both are handled explicitly rather than avoided.
EXECUTION CHARACTER SET, PINNED AT TRANSLATION TIME. This program prints the numeric codes of the
characters in its literals, and C11 5.2.1 leaves those values to the implementation -- nothing in
the standard says 'a' is 97. All four supported targets were measured to use ASCII, and the source
pins the characters it prints with two _Static_assert declarations: one conjunctive assertion naming
fifteen letters (a, b, c, d, e, h, i, l, o, r, t, w, x, y and z at their ASCII codes) and one for the
simple escape '\n' == 10. An implementation with a different execution character set therefore fails
to TRANSLATE rather than printing different numbers that an oracle would have to attribute to a
compiler defect. Verified by negative test: perturbing one asserted value makes translation stop with
the assertion's own message.
THE PINNING COVERS FIFTEEN OF THE SEVENTEEN LETTERS THIS PROGRAM PRINTS, NOT ALL SEVENTEEN, and the
gap is stated here rather than rounded up, because a guarantee claimed wider than it is costs a
reader more than one described exactly. 'f' (102) and 'g' (103) are printed and are not named by the
conjunction; they occur only in the two automatic-duration twins, l_exact_u from "def" at index 2 and
l_room from "gh" at index 0, which is why they are easy to miss. Neither is implied by its
neighbours: C11 5.2.1 guarantees contiguity only for the digits, so 'e' == 101 and 'h' == 104 pin
nothing between them. The residual is bounded and reaches no oracle in this environment -- all
four targets were measured ASCII and all twelve configurations printed 102 and 103 -- but on a
hypothetical non-ASCII target this program would translate and then print two byte values that
differed for a conforming reason, and l_exact_u[2] and l_room[0] are exactly the two golden lines
where that would surface. Note also that the assertion's own message says "every letter this program
prints", which claims more than the conjunction delivers; this field is the accurate statement of the
two. Closing the gap belongs in the program, by adding 'f' == 102 && 'g' == 103 to the conjunction --
a change that alters no output at all, because a _Static_assert emits none -- and it must revise this
paragraph and that message in the same change. It is recorded rather than performed here because
the program is not this record's to edit and the residual blocks nothing.
The two NUMERIC escapes in g_escapes need no assertion -- C11 6.4.4.4p7 fixes \x41 and \102 at 65
and 66 by their numeric value regardless of charset, which is precisely why they are spelled
numerically -- whereas the simple escape \n does need one, because 6.4.4.4p4 gives it a
charset-dependent value.
PLAIN-CHAR SIGNEDNESS. Plain char is never printed as itself. Its signedness is
implementation-defined and was MEASURED signed on x86-64 and i686 but unsigned on AArch64 and
RISC-V 64, so a plain-char value printed as itself would diverge across backends for a reason that
is not a compiler defect -- which would destroy the oracle rather than exercise it. Two mechanisms
are applied together so the rule holds by construction and cannot be broken by a later edit that
introduces a high-bit character. Every array the requirement names is spelled unsigned char or
signed char and read back through (int), whose value is unambiguous by declaration. Every
plain-char object, and the const char * dereference, is read back through (int)(unsigned char),
which the dump_plain helper exists solely to centralise so it cannot be forgotten at a call site.
Each cast is a value conversion of an already-loaded character, never pointer punning. Every
literal in the file is US-ASCII, so in practice the two spellings agree; the cast is applied
unconditionally anyway.
WIDTH NORMALIZATION. Every printed value is an int printed with %d, and every character value is
cast before printing. Each sizeof is taken of a CHARACTER array, so its value is a count of bytes
and is identical on all four targets -- 3, 4, 6, 7, 10 and 12 -- and is cast to int before it
reaches the variadic call. sizeof is deliberately never taken of g_ptr, whose width differs
between the targets: i686 is ELF32 with a four-byte pointer while x86-64, AArch64 and RISC-V 64
are ELF64 with eight (docs/technical-specifications.md target table, lines 457-462), a difference
also MEASURED on all four targets in this environment. No long, size_t, ptrdiff_t, intptr_t, float
or double appears and no length modifier is used. No address or pointer VALUE is printed -- the
pointer case is reported through its pointee, not its address -- so where the implementation places
these objects, and whether two identical literals share storage, is unobservable.
PADDING. Character arrays have no padding, and no object representation is inspected.
REPORT SAFETY OF THE GOLDEN BYTES. One byte of the escape-sequence array would be a control
character if it were rendered: g_escapes is initialized from "\x41\102\n", whose third element is a
line feed. It is read through dump_u as a NUMBER and prints as 10, which is required rather than
incidental -- the expectation record format permits no control character but the line feed that
joins its own lines, because a field value is copied verbatim into a report row, a tab-separated
summary column and a findings manifest. Reading the escapes numerically covers them fully and keeps
the golden bytes report-safe at the same time.
THE EXACT-WIDTH DECLARATIONS MUST NEVER BE "FIXED" BY WIDENING THEM; they are the construct under
test. g_grid_exact and g_grid_room differ only in row width, which makes the terminator's presence
or absence a single-line difference between two otherwise identical groups, and that is the
comparison this program exists to make. The exact sizes are the lines that would detect a compiler
reserving a byte it was not asked for.
THE DISCRIMINATING VARIANT in this area is the pairing of storage durations rather than a volatile
operand. The two automatic-duration objects, l_exact_u and l_room, are the twins: they carry the
exact-width and with-room forms into the path where the initializer is performed by emitted code at
run time rather than laid into a data section at translation time. With optimization off the statics
appear as data-section bytes while the twins appear as a run of stores into the frame, and at a
higher level either path may be folded into immediate operands, so both are compiled at all three
levels rather than at one and their answers are printed separately. A volatile operand would add
nothing, because the subject here is what an initializer produces rather than what an operator
computes.
MEASURED EVIDENCE. The program was compiled with the reference driver for all four targets at -O0,
-O1 and -O2 -- twelve configurations -- and every one produced these 87 lines byte-identically with
exit status 0, which is why oracle (b) compares this program's targets against each other without
any narrowing and why oracle (c) can hold these bytes as the golden record. What would legitimately
break that is a target whose execution character set is not ASCII, such as one using EBCDIC: it
would print different byte values without being defective, and that would be an
implementation-defined divergence to record as an expected divergence with this record's target list
narrowed and the reason stated in this field -- never left to surface as a finding against a backend
that did nothing wrong, and never resolved by dropping the string-literal initializer coverage
this program exists to provide.
END
